-- title:
--   Degree-one η-sections at the node of the standard edge
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\bar k = W(k)/pW(k)$ the quotient of the Witt ring by the ideal `pIdeal` generated by $p$; $\iota$ is a ring homomorphism from $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ (written `Zp2 p`) to $W(k)$, and `jbar ι` denotes the induced character $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2} \to \bar k$, namely $\iota$ followed by reduction. A `FormalODModule p B` consists of a two-dimensional commutative formal group law $F$ over $B$ together with an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ by law endomorphisms and a law endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$. For such an $X$ and a character $j$, the graded piece `X.gradedPiece j n` is the subgroup of the Cartier module $\mathrm{Cart}_p(X.F)$ of those $f$ with $\mathrm{act}(\tau(c)) \cdot f = j(\tau(c))^{p^n} f$ (homothety) for every $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$, $\tau$ the Teichmüller lift; when the pieces $0$ and $1$ are complementary, `toGradedCartierModuleData` packages $\mathrm{Cart}_p(X.F)$ with its Frobenius $F$, Verschiebung $V$, the operator $\Pi$ induced by $\varpi$, and this grading, and `NMod` is the associated quotient module, with `nMk : M × M →+ NMod`, `nVarpi` the induced $\Pi$, `vRange` the image of $V$, and `etaPiece L hL i` the intersection of the subgroup `eta L` with the degree-$i$ part.
--
--   The data and hypotheses on $\Phi$ are: $\Phi$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $\bar k$; `hΦ` says $\Phi$ is special for `jbar ι`, i.e. `lieZero` and `lieOne` are complementary in the tangent space and both invertible; `hΦ4` says $\mathrm{act}(p)$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ` says `lieZero (jbar ι)` is contained in the kernel of `lieVarpi`, the linear part of $\varpi$ acting on the tangent space; `hcΦ` says the graded pieces $0$ and $1$ of $\Phi$ for `jbar ι` are complementary; $r_\Phi$ is an additive map from $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ to the `NMod` of $\Phi$'s graded Cartier module data, and `hrΦ` requires that for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on that data (a Cartier $L$-map admitting a lift over a $p$-torsion-free surjection from a special graded Cartier module), $r_\Phi$ restricts to a bijection from all of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ onto `etaPiece L … 0`. Further, $g$ is an element of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ with `hg` asserting that its underlying matrix is $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$.
--
--   The data and hypotheses on the edge family are: $X$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over the characteristic-$p$ edge-chart ring $E =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p` $\bar k$, i.e. `FormalOmega.chartERing` of $\bar k$ at $0$, and $\gamma : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathrm{Cart}_p(X.F)$; the relevant character is $\iota$ followed by $\bar k \to E$ composed with reduction. Then `hγ` says $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis: $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece $i$ and the determinant of the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_l)$ is a unit; `hγa` says $\gamma$ has the edge structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p` $\bar k$ (the constants built from $\xi$ and $\eta$), i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ there is $h$ with $\varpi\gamma_i = \sum_{m<N} V^m(a_{m,i}\gamma_{\pi(m,i)}) + V^N h$; `hXs` says $X$ is special for that character and `hX4` that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   The node point and the isogeny: $f_0 : E \to \bar k$ is a ring homomorphism with `hf₀` a retraction condition ($f_0$ composed after the structure map $\bar k \to E$ is the identity) and `hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η` saying $f_0(\xi) = f_0(\eta) = 0$; $m$ is a natural number and $\rho_0$ a $2$-tuple of power series over $\bar k$ with `hρ₀` asserting that $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi \to X \otimes_{f_0} \bar k$ of height $4m$, that is, an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Write $t_0$ for the rigidified triple `Rigidified.mk` with module $X \otimes_{f_0}\bar k$, integer $n = m$, and rigidification the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. The hypothesis `hOD₀` says that the rigidification of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar X$ for $t_0$; `hcb` says the graded pieces $0$ and $1$ of the reduction $\bar X$ of $t_0$ along the identity of $\bar k$ are complementary, and `hcΦg` the same for the corresponding reduction of $\Phi$. Finally $a$ is a natural number and `hN` is the normalisation of the rigidification numerator at the node: for each $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$,
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota, h_{c\Phi}, r_\Phi, \ldots)(\mathrm{Pi.single}\ i\ 1) = p^{a+m} \cdot w_i,$$
--   where `rigidNum` is $r_\Phi$ followed by the base-change map on `NMod`s induced by $\Phi \to \bar\Phi$ and then by the map induced by the rigidification, and where $w_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\hat\gamma_0, 0)$ and $w_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}(\mathrm{nMk}(\hat\gamma_1, 0))$, with $\hat\gamma_i$ the image of $\gamma_i$ under base change along $f_0$, then along reduction modulo $p$ of $\bar k$, then along `reduceMap` of the identity.
--
--   Lastly, $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra and $y_0 : \bar k \to \Omega$ a ring homomorphism; $t_0 \otimes_{y_0} \Omega$ denotes the pushforward `Rigidified.map y₀` of $t_0$, and `Rigidified.awayHom (1 : Ω)` the structure homomorphism of $\Omega$ into the ring attached to the basic open set $D(1)$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of: a proof `hOD` that the rigidification of $t_0 \otimes_{y_0}\Omega$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar X$ for that triple (with character $y_0$ after reduction); proofs `hc`, `hcb`, `hcΦg` that the graded pieces $0$ and $1$ are complementary for, respectively, the module $X_S$ of $t_0\otimes_{y_0}\Omega$ over $D(1)$ with character `Rigidified.jS`, its reduction $\bar X_S$ with character `Rigidified.jSbar`, and the corresponding reduction of $\Phi$; an additive map $L$ from $M$ to `NMod` for the graded Cartier module data $D$ of $X_S$ determined by `hc`, together with a proof `hL` that $L$ is a canonical $L$-map; elements $z_0, z_1$ of $D$'s `NMod` with `hz₀`, `hz₁` asserting that each is an $\eta$-section in degree $1$ — that is, $z_0 \in$ `etaPiece L … 1` and the reduction to $\bar X_S$ of $\Pi z_0$ stands in the relation `LatticeRel` (with parameter $n = m$) to the rigidification numerator of $t_0 \otimes_{y_0}\Omega$ at the vector $p \cdot (p^{-1} e_1)$, where $e_1 =$ `stdBasisVec ℚ_[p] 1`, and likewise $z_1 \in$ `etaPiece L … 1` with the corresponding relation at $p\cdot e_0$, $e_0 =$ `stdBasisVec ℚ_[p] 0`; and elements $m_0, m_1$ of $D.M$ such that the following four conditions hold.
--
--   First, the class of $m_0$ modulo `vRange` (the image of $V$) equals $u(L)$ applied to $z_0$, viewed as an element of the subgroup `eta L` by the first clause of `hz₀`. Second, the class of $m_1$ modulo `vRange` equals $u(L)$ applied to $z_1$, viewed in `eta L` by the first clause of `hz₁`. Third, for every $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$,
--   $$\mathrm{tangent}(m_1)_j = -\bigl(\mathrm{Rigidified.awayHom}\,(1 : \Omega) \text{ applied to } 0\bigr)\cdot \mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j,$$
--   so that the scalar is the image of $0$ and the tangent vector of $m_1$ vanishes identically. Fourth, there exists $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$ with $\mathrm{tangent}(m_0)_j \neq 0$. Here $\mathrm{tangent}$ is the additive map sending a Cartier module element to the tuple of coefficients of the first linear monomial in its component power series.
--
--   This is the local computation at the node of the standard edge in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd theory of special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules: at the node both graded indices are critical, and the statement produces degree-one $\eta$-sections presenting $p^{-1}e_1$ and $e_0$ together with representatives whose tangent vectors are, respectively, zero and non-zero. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.ker_eq_span_of_lattice_eq_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq), which identifies the kernel of the associated lattice map at the node.
